      {
        "review_id": 527720699,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r527720699-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Sacred place that needs a lot of attention",
        "text": "Inspite of the fact that this place is a UNESCO site, the upkeep and maintenance does not really tell so. It is sad to see temples with such heritage and importance being kept so badly. If one wants to understand the temple better it is always better to engage a knowledgeable person who knows the history very well.  Only then will the visit to the temple be useful.   There is so much to be told as a review will not just suffice.  However in a nutshell this is one of the places where the Pandavas went in search of Shiva seeking salvation.  The name of the temple comes from the fact that a Cow (Pasu) used to offer daily prayers by offering its own milk over the Shivalina.  The Shivalingam is Suyambu, and there are four faces that darshan has to be performed.  The four face darshan is available only in the morning after 9.30 AM.  One can wait till late evening 7.30 PM once again to see all the four faces.  Generally there is a huge rush to see this darshan.  There are several small shrines inside the temple complex.  Most important among them is the Mukthisthal.  According to the Tibetian/Nepalis custom in the yesteryears a person who wishes to attain Mukthi will come here and merge with the river flowing below the temple, in the presence of the Guru.  Even today you see people performing this in a slightly different way, where the final rites are performed on the river side in a particular way.  The temple is a very powerful one, provided one is receptive to the energy that is available.  This is a tantric center and is indeed very powerful when one sits in deep meditation.  One can definitely feel the raw energy in the place.  There is a Rudraksha tree inside the temple complex that should not be missed. Rudraksha is considered as the tear of Lord Shiva.  The Rudra Abishey is also very famous here.  You would need to hire a priest to perform this in the morning time.  Better to go with a know person.",
