      {
        "review_id": 525662648,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r525662648-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Serene",
        "text": "The Diety is said to represent the head aspect of Shiva. The legend goes that when Pandavas chased Shiva who in the form of a bull was trying to escape from them, the body exploded. The hump fell at Kedarnath while the head fell at Pashupatinath.\nSituated on the banks of Bhagmati river which is the Ganges of Nepal, it reminds you of Varanasi \nThe inner sanctum of the temple has four doors, the west door is mostly open while the remaining doors open during specific hours. If wanting to do an abhishekam, you can get a ticket at the SBI bank counter in temple premises. Don't go by touts who say that you can't get tickets for the same day. You get priority entry from the east gate and see the diety at close quarters and the chance to do Aarthi.\nLike Varanasi there are cremations happening at the rear of the temple along the banks of Bhagmati river and also an evening Aarthi by riverside. \nThere are many small temples within the complex. Photography is not allowed once through the main entrance of the temple. Entry to non Hindus is restricted.",
